# Executing specifications over bitvector streams.
#
# See demos/README.md for how to run this script.

# demo_2.3 solved bitvector equations; this demo runs them. Streams may carry
# bitvectors like any other Boolean algebra value: declare the stream with a
# bv[n] type. A bitvector value on a file or console stream is written as a
# plain number, one per line, with no braces and no trailing period --
# fixture/bv8-length_3.in holds the three values 200, 100 and 7.

# Widths never mix silently (demo_2.3), and that holds across streams too: an
# 8-bit input can only meet a 16-bit output through an explicit cast. This
# program widens each input into a larger register, exactly the shape that
# GitHub issues #86-#88 were about. As in demo_3.1, the run stops asking for
# input once the file is exhausted, and q at the continue prompt ends it:

i1:bv[8] := in file("fixture/bv8-length_3.in").
o1:bv[16] := out console.
r o1[t] = (bv[16]) i1[t]
q

# Arithmetic in a specification is modular, wrapping at the stream's width. The
# accumulator below sums its inputs in 8 bits: 200, then 200+100 = 300, which
# wraps to 44, then 44+7 = 51. Note the initial condition pinning o2[0]; the
# recurrence with its t-1 lookback only binds from step 1 onwards. A file
# stream is not rewound, so reading the same file again takes a fresh stream:

i2:bv[8] := in file("fixture/bv8-length_3.in").
o2:bv[8] := out console.
r o2[0] = i2[0] && o2[t] = o2[t-1] + i2[t]
q

# The wrap above is silent. To saturate instead -- clamp at 255 and stay
# there -- use the builtin min: o3[t-1]' is the bitwise complement, i.e.
# 255 - o3[t-1], so min(i3[t], o3[t-1]') is the largest addend that cannot
# push the sum past 255 (the README's "checked and saturating arithmetic"
# section derives this and its subtraction dual). Same inputs as above:
# 200, then 200 + 100 = 300 clamps to 255, then 255 + 7 stays 255:

i3:bv[8] := in file("fixture/bv8-length_3.in").
o3:bv[8] := out console.
r o3[0] = i3[0] && o3[t] = o3[t-1] + min(i3[t], o3[t-1]')
q

# When wrapping is not wanted, compute wide and narrow the result: the
# widen-multiply-divide-narrow idiom. Taking 5% of an 8-bit value by
# multiplying first would wrap (200 * 5 = 1000, far beyond 255); casting up to
# 16 bits first keeps the product exact, and the quotient fits back into 8
# bits. The outputs below are 5% of 200, 100 and 7 -- 10, 5 and 0:

i4:bv[8] := in file("fixture/bv8-length_3.in").
o4:bv[8] := out console.
r o4[t] = (bv[8]) ( ((bv[16]) i4[t] * {5}:bv[16]) / {100}:bv[16] )
q

# Bitvector streams can also read from the console. The prompt names the
# stream, the time point and the expected type, and the value is again a bare
# number. This program halves its input. It is constrained at time point 0
# only, so after the supplied value is consumed, step 1 needs no input and
# emits an unconstrained default 0; q then ends the run at the continue
# prompt:

i5:bv[8] := in console.
o5:bv[8] := out console.
r o5[0] = i5[0] / {2}:bv[8]
90
q

quit
